.SS Database lifecycle
Back up, restore and refresh the database itself; export and import the
signed pair (dump + bundle) that moves it to another machine.
.TP
\f[B]export <strategy> <dump> <bundle>\f[R]
Write the two artifacts that travel apart, bound and signed: the DUMP to
a path of your choosing (commit it), the BUNDLE to its store.
The strategy is the first word: redacted (no credentials, for sending) |
full (own machines, disaster recovery) | minimized (rescue: the
configurations and the key, no receipts).
Every strategy describes the database completely and governs the payload
only
.TP
\f[B]restore <dump> [<base>]\f[R]
Replay a dump into the current database \(en only a dump signed by the
PINNED attest key, at or above the lineage\(cqs serial; <base> binds
where its base elements come from (default: this database).
The dump\(cqs format (its `# Version:' header) selects the restore that
speaks it: `restore v<format> <dump> <base>', which reads the serial,
the pinned key, the signer and the signer\(cqs floor once and hands them
down \(en into a database that does not yet hold the records (an
identical import is refused as `already exists', the rest replays)
.TP
\f[B]filter <collection> <filtered>\f[R]
Apply the default strategy to a collection (delegates: default \->
redacted)
.TP
\f[B]filter redacted <collection> <filtered>\f[R]
Drop what is a secret: the VPN configurations themselves
(wireguard/\f[I].conf, openvpn/\f[R].ovpn carry keys and credentials)
\(en and always the operational directories.
The description still travels complete (categories, links, sessions,
keys, receipts); what a bug report or a second machine\(cqs skeleton
needs, without the credentials.
The collection is readable: rewrite to `filter write redacted \&...',
else an error line
.TP
\f[B]filter full <collection> <filtered>\f[R]
Keep everything except the operational directories: the configurations
with their keys travel.
For one\(cqs own machines (a rescue medium, a second host), never for
sending to strangers.
The collection is readable: rewrite to `filter write full \&...', else
an error line
.TP
\f[B]filter minimized <collection> <filtered>\f[R]
Keep what connecting needs only: the configurations (wireguard/,
openvpn/) and the attest key; no receipts.
What a rescue system needs to come online, nothing of the lineage.
The collection is readable: rewrite to `filter write minimized \&...',
else an error line
.TP
\f[B]bundle <collection> <archive>\f[R]
Pack the collected files with VPN_SWITCH_ARCHIVER: the collection is
readable, the archiver set, the archive path absolute, the manifest pair
(MANIFEST + MANIFEST.asc) lies beside the collection \(en an archive
that carries no tamper detection is not an artifact this tool produces
\(en and the collection lives inside the database (the pair is packed by
its database\-relative path); then `bundle pack'
.TP
\f[B]seal <dump> <bundle>\f[R]
Append the bundle\(cqs sha256 and size to the dump as its seal line (`#
Bundle: sha256=\&...
bytes=\&...'): both files readable, the dump not yet sealed (a dump
names ONE bundle) and not yet attested (sealing after signing would
invalidate the signature); then `seal write'.
Attest the dump AFTER sealing so one signature covers both
.TP
\f[B]seal verify <dump> <bundle>\f[R]
Check that the dump\(cqs seal line names THIS bundle (sha256 and size):
both files readable, the dump sealed, the seal matching \(en a mismatch
fails the command before anything is extracted
.TP
\f[B]incoming clear <directory>\f[R]
Empty one scratch directory under $VPN_SWITCH_ROOT/incoming/ \(en
import\(cqs own unpacking area, cleared before each extract so a
re\-import never trips over the last one.
The path lies below incoming/ and carries no `..': rewrite to `incoming
remove', else an error line (only import\(cqs own scratch is cleared
here, never a database)
.TP
\f[B]extract <archive> <destination>\f[R]
Unpack an archive into a scratch directory with VPN_SWITCH_EXTRACTOR:
the archive readable, the extractor set, the destination absolute and
empty (or not yet there); then `extract unpack'
.TP
\f[B]import <dump> <bundle>\f[R]
Replay an exported pair into the current database, checked end to end
BEFORE anything lands, cheapest first, every detail read ONCE on the way
in and handed down: the dump\(cqs serial, the pinned signer, the
dump\(cqs signature (gpg once), the signer\(cqs floor, this
database\(cqs serial; then `import <dump> <bundle> <serial> <key> <fpr>
<floor> <cur>' \(en the seal (this bundle is the one the dump names),
the bundle unpacked into import\(cqs own scratch under incoming/, its
MANIFEST (pinned signer, every hash), the receipt filed BEFORE the
replay (the replay runs keep\-going, so a redacted pair\(cqs withheld
elements cost no receipt), the replay.
ONLY into an EMPTY database (a fresh bootstrap with the attest key
pinned): on a database that already holds the configs the replay reports
them as existing and goes on
.TP
\f[B]attest <file> <key>\f[R]
Sign a file with a registered openpgp key (armored detached signature
\-> <file>.asc): the file exists, the record exists and carries its
keyid; then `attest sign'
.TP
\f[B]attest verify <file> <key>\f[R]
Check a detached signature at generation time \(en signed by the PINNED
key, key neither expired nor revoked: the file exists, the record exists
and is complete; then `attest signer pinned' (a finding fails the
command)
.TP
\f[B]dump\f[R]
Export the database as an executable shell script: the export serial
read here, then `dump <serial>' \(en the header (version, date, serial),
the environment prologue, the keys, every protocol, the sessions, the
receipts, the environment epilogue.
Import lines reference base elements against
\(lq$VPN_SWITCH_ARCHIVE_BASE\(rq; `export' pairs the dump with the
bundle that carries them
.TP
\f[B]batch <file>\f[R]
Execute a file of vpn\-switch commands line by line
.TP
\f[B]env sync\f[R]
Refresh environment defaults in .env/default/ from source templates
.TP
\f[B]version sync\f[R]
Stamp the database\(cqs .version with the source\(cqs current SHA
.TP
\f[B]manifest <collection> <manifest>\f[R]
Hash every entry of a collection into a manifest, (LC_ALL=C\-sorted
`path sha256=hash', symlinks as `path symlink=target'): the collection
is readable, the manifest is named MANIFEST (import looks for it by
name, next to the collection), every entry is hashable; then `manifest
write' \(en everything hashed at generation, the emission is one
concrete heredoc write
.TP
\f[B]manifest attest <collection> <key>\f[R]
Write the manifest for a collection and sign it, side by side with the
collection: manifest + attest
.TP
\f[B]manifest verify <manifest> <base> <key>\f[R]
Verify a manifest, both judgments at generation time: the signature
(attest verify: signed by the PINNED key, key neither expired nor
revoked) and the tree (manifest match: every listed entry present and
identical) \(en a finding fails the batch before restore replays
anything.
The key is the RECEIVER\(cqs openpgp record naming the signer it expects
.TP
\f[B]manifest match <manifest> <base>\f[R]
Does the tree under <base> match the manifest?
The manifest is readable and lists entries, the base exists; then
`manifest tree matches' \(en every listed file hashes identically, every
listed symlink points where recorded (MISSING, CHANGED, RETARGETED
fail).
Files present but unlisted are not findings: the base is an extraction
directory
.TP
\f[B]phases sync [<phase>]\f[R]
Refresh phase scripts (firewall, vpn, dns) from source templates
.TP
\f[B]provenance serial\f[R]
Advance the export serial by one: the number read here, then `provenance
serial <current>' \(en export does this LAST, once the pair is attested:
the dump header already carries current+1, so a failed export leaves the
number to the next attempt (serials were once lost to a pinentry that
could not open)
.TP
\f[B]provenance add <dump> <bundle>\f[R]
File the receipt of an admitted import.
Every detail read ONCE on the way in and handed down \(en the dump\(cqs
serial, the pinned key, the signer (gpg once), the signer\(cqs floor,
this database\(cqs serial \(en then `provenance add <dump> <bundle>
<serial> <key> <fpr> <floor> <cur>': the bundle readable or `\-', the
serial not a downgrade, `provenance receipt' (files it, or says it is
filed, or refuses a differing one) and the export serial raised to the
imported one
.TP
\f[B]provenance import <id> <absfile>\f[R]
Copy ONE file of a receipt record from another database (dump/restore
base element; the record directory is created on first file): the id is
a record name, the source exists; then `provenance import place'
.TP
\f[B]provenance dump\f[R]
Emit the provenance portion of a database dump: one `provenance import'
line per receipt file (cat\-pinned: dump TEXT, replayed by restore);
none is a comment line
.TP
\f[B]provenance collect\f[R]
Text terminal: the receipt record files that belong into an archive, by
their real path against \(lq$VPN_SWITCH_ARCHIVE_BASE\(rq
.TP
\f[B]provenance list\f[R]
The export serial and every receipt: the serial read here, then
`provenance list <serial>' renders
.SS Keys
The attest key: an openpgp record names the GnuPG key that signs what
this database exports and the signer a receiver expects (pinned).
.TP
\f[B]openpgp add <name> <keyid> [<gnupghome>]\f[R]
Register a GnuPG attest key; gnupghome for a keyring living elsewhere:
the name is a record name, the keyid is hex; then `openpgp record'
.TP
\f[B]openpgp dump\f[R]
Emit the openpgp portion of a database dump: one `openpgp add' replay
line per registered key, in the arity of the record (cat\-pinned: dump
TEXT, replayed by `restore'), plus one `openpgp import' per extra file;
none is a comment line
.TP
\f[B]openpgp import <name> <absfile>\f[R]
Copy ONE extra file into the key record \(en dump/restore base element
(the schema files travel as the `openpgp add' replay): rewrite to `key
import openpgp <name> <absfile>'
.TP
\f[B]openpgp collect\f[R]
List the files of the openpgp key records that belong into an archive
\(en public material only; private key material stays a path promise
into the world: one `openpgp collect <key>' per record, none is a
comment line
